# 200 — Architecture layer in the kernel and libmind (x86-64 first) — done

**Type:** kernel · **Owner:** porting track (with the kernel owner) · **Priority:** P2 · **Status:** done (2026-10-05) · **Blocked by:** — · **Roadmap:** track H · **Constitution:** MC-12.1 (profile per platform)

## Problem

The kernel mixes platform code with the rest:
- interrupt descriptor table, APIC, 8259/PIT;
- x86 page tables;
- context-switch assembly, `int 0x80`;
- the SMP trampoline, ACPI reset, port I/O for PCI, CPUID/RDTSC.

libmind does the same with its system-call stub, RDRAND and RDTSC. Another architecture cannot be added without first drawing the line.

## Plan

- `kernel/src/arch/x86_64/` with a small interface the rest of the kernel uses, and the generic kernel calls only that:
  - entry and exceptions, interrupt controller and timer;
  - address-space operations (map, unmap, protect, switch, TLB shootdown);
  - context save/restore and user entry;
  - CPU start, reset and power;
  - the clock source, the entropy source, the platform bus (PCI configuration access).
- `libmind/src/arch/x86_64.rs`: the system-call instruction, the cycle counter, the entropy instruction.
- `common/abi.rs` keeps only what every architecture shares. Architecture-specific constants move next to their code.
- x86 legacy drivers (`LEGACY:`) stay x86-only and are built only there.
- No change in behavior: every QEMU suite and host test passes unchanged, and the boot log is the same.

## Acceptance criteria

- `cargo build` of the kernel and libmind only reaches x86 code through `arch::`. A grep for `asm!` outside `arch/` finds nothing.
- All CI jobs green.
- `docs/profile/tcb.md` names the architecture layer in the TCB.

## Related

[201](../issues/201-aarch64-boot.md), [ROADMAP](../ROADMAP.md) track H.

## Resolution

- **Kernel:** `kernel/src/arch/mod.rs` selects the architecture; `kernel/src/arch/x86_64/` holds `acpi`, `clock`, `context`, `cpu`, `interrupts`, `paging`, `pci`, and the new `port` (port I/O, the drivers' port reads and writes) and `serial` (COM1). The generic kernel (`main.rs`, `scheduler`, `frames`, `memory`, `user_heap`, `elf`, `input`, `task_state`) names them through `crate::arch` imports and calls processor operations as functions: `cpu::cycles`, `cpu::fault_address`, `cpu::reschedule`, `cpu::wait_for_interrupt`, `cpu::disable_interrupts`, `cpu::halt_here`, `interrupts::pic_eoi`, `port::read`/`write`. No `asm!`, `global_asm!` or `core::arch::x86_64` is left outside `arch/`.
- **libmind:** `libmind/src/arch/` with `trap` (the system-call instruction, used only by `sys::syscall`) and `entropy_available`/`entropy` (RDRAND, used by `random`). The host tests that include `sys.rs` include the arch module too.
- **Not moved:** `common/abi.rs` keeps the x86-only parts of the ABI (port system calls, `PLATFORM_PORTS`, PIC line numbers) for now: they are part of the system-call interface every program and driver is built against, so they move when 201 defines what aarch64 offers instead. The x86 legacy drivers are user programs; building them only for x86 starts with the first aarch64 build (201/202).
- **No change in behavior:** every QEMU suite (including `--cpu-model max`) and the host tests pass; the boot log is the same.
- `docs/profile/tcb.md` names the architecture layer; `docs/legacy.md` points at its new paths.
